

## 4 Elementary Transcendental Functions

Logarithmic, Exponential, Circular and Hyperbolic Functions

**Mathematical Properties**

**4.1. Logarithmic Function**

**Integral Representation**

**4.1.1**

\[\ln z{=}{\int_{1}^{z}}\frac{dt}{t}\]

where the path of integration does not pass through the origin or cross the negative real axis. In \(z\) is a single-valued function, regular in the \(z\)-plane cut along the negative real axis, real when \(z\) is positive.

\[z{=}x{+}iy{=}re^{iq}.\]

**4.1.2**

\[\ln z{=}{\ln}~{}r{+}i\theta\quad(-\pi{<}\theta{\leq}\pi).\]

**4.1.3**

\(r{=}(x^{2}{+}y^{2})^{\frac{1}{2}}\), \(x{=}r\cos\theta\), \(y{=}r\sin\theta\), \(\theta{=}\arctan\frac{y}{x}\)

The general logarithmic function is the many-valued function Ln \(z\) defined by

**4.1.4**

\[\mbox{Ln}~{}z{=}{\int_{1}^{z}}\frac{dt}{t}\]

where the path does not pass through the origin.

**4.1.5**

\[\mbox{Ln}~{}\langle re^{iq}\rangle{=}\ln\langle re^{q}\rangle{+}2k\pi i{=}\ln ~{}r{+}i(\theta{+}2k\pi),\]

\(k\) being an arbitrary integer. In \(z\) is said to be the _principal branch_ of Ln \(z\).

